  \ar[dd]_*{\alpha'}
& &
 A
  \ar[rd]^*{\gamma}
  \ar[dd]^(.4)*{\alpha}
\\ &
 B'
  \ar[ld]^*{\beta'}
  \ar[rr] |{\hole} ^(.3)*{h_\beta}
& &
 B
  \ar[ld]^*{\beta}
\\
 C'
  \ar[rr]_*{h}
& &
 C
}\end{xy}$
and that the two squares in it are pullback diagrams.
Then there is a unique morphism $\gamma': A' \to B'$ making the following commute:
$\begin{xy}\xymatrix@+1em@L+2px{
 A'
  \ar[rr]^*{h_\alpha}
  \ar[dd]_*{\alpha'}
  \ar@{-->}[rd]^*{\gamma'}
& &
 A
  \ar[rd]^*{\gamma}
  \ar[dd]^(.4)*{\alpha}
\\ &
 B'
  \ar[ld]^*{\beta'}
  \ar[rr] |{\hole} ^(.3)*{h_\beta}
& &
 B
  \ar[ld]^*{\beta}
\\
 C'
  \ar[rr]_*{h}
& &
 C
}\end{xy}$
Furthermore, $\gamma'$ makes the following a pullback:
$\begin{xy}\xymatrix@+.5em@L+2px{
 A'
  \ar[r]^*{h_\alpha}
  \ar[d]_*{\gamma'}
&
 A
  \ar[d]^*{\gamma}
\\
 B'
  \ar[r]_*{h_\beta}
&
 B
}\end{xy}$

proof_text:
The first diagram above can be distorted into:
$\begin{xy}\xymatrix@+.5em@L+2px{
 A'
  \ar@/^/[rrd]^*{\gamma \circ h_\alpha}
  \ar@/_/[ddr]_*{\alpha'}
\\ &
 B'
  \ar[r]^*{h_\beta}
  \ar[d]^*{\beta'}
&
 B
  \ar[d]^*{\beta}
\\ &
 C'
  \ar[r]_*{h}
&
 C
}\end{xy}$
and since the square is a pullback, there is a unique $\gamma': A' \to B'$ as desired.
That:
$\begin{xy}\xymatrix@+.5em@L+2px{
 A'
  \ar[r]^*{h_\alpha}
  \ar[d]_*{\gamma'}
&
 A
  \ar[d]^*{\gamma}
\\
 B'
  \ar[r]_*{h_\beta}
&
 B
}\end{xy}$
is a pullback follows immediately from the Pullback Lemma.

Let $\alpha: A \to D$ be an object of $\mathbf C \mathbin / D$.
Then the identity morphism $\operatorname{id}_\alpha: \alpha \to \alpha$ is by definition $\operatorname{id}_A: A \to A$.
Thus $f^* \operatorname{id}_\alpha$ is by definition the unique morphism fitting:
$\begin{xy}\xymatrix@+1em{
 A'
  \ar[rr]^*{f_\alpha}
